{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428973,"nonce":"F7m7L2k0ChBM0Xv3ymRezq2qr9c=","message":"Fixed. amroutes.go now walks the full route tree with Alertmanager's own semantics — inheritance from the nearest ancestor that states each timing (carrying the DEPTH that stated it, so '5m because this route says so' stays distinct from '5m because the root says so'), first-match-wins with continue, delivery only where no child matched, and match/match_re normalised to matchers so a match:-only route does not look matcher-less and wrongly shadow its sibling. A matcher-less child without continue marks later siblings unreachable — the only unreachability provable without labels. Caps at 64 routes / 16 levels with a visible dropped count. Stored by 00037, served on route_timings as routes[].\n\nTHE ANSWER IS A SET AND IS NEVER COLLAPSED: several routes can reach oto with different timings, so the UI says 'N routes reach your oto receiver and they disagree' rather than silently picking one. route is oto_receiver only when a receiver was named AND every reaching route agrees; otherwise it falls back to top_level with routes_agree:false.\n\nWhich receiver is oto's CANNOT BE READ: Alertmanager redacts webhook_config.url as a SecretURL, so config.original returns the literal \u003csecret\u003e — confirmed against the checked-in real capture. Identification is therefore a labelled inference (receiver_basis: sole_webhook / ambiguous / no_webhook / unknown), and sole_webhook answers the common install exactly, since that is the shape SPEC §J.1 tells everyone to deploy.\n\nManual entry and the localStorage copy are gone. 00028 had already done the top-level part and declined the tree; this is a pure additive widening beside it.","files":null}]}